<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morice Line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Morice Line was a fortified barrier built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1956 along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, named after the defence minister André Morice. Made of electrified fences, minefields and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a>, backed by mobile troops, it cut the FLN's fighters inside Algeria off from their bases and supplies in Tunisia. A similar line ran along the Moroccan border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morice-line.jpg" alt="Morice Line" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saber68 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligne_Morice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The barrier was built under French authority during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> to stop supplies from neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> inside French-controlled Algeria. It went into effect in September 1957. Though not a fortification in the traditional sense, it was equipped with what were then state-of-the-art electronic systems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its centre was an electric fence 2.5 metres high that ran its entire length, carried 5,000 volts and had barbed-wire entanglements on one side. A minefield extended 45 metres on each side of the fence, and on the Algerian side a patrolled track ran beside it. Along the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> it ran for 460 km, and a further 700 km of barrier followed the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Alarms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radars</a> and searchlights, together with anti-personnel mines, allowed the forces assigned to the line to coordinate their response, and the combination made it almost impenetrable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The line sharply reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> activity by forces coming from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and proved effective in limiting the FLN's operations during the war. After the war ended in 1962, the new Algerian authorities set out to clear and dismantle it, in part by forcing captured harkis and other Muslim loyalists who had served with the French Army to do the work, and many of them were killed by mines.</p><p>For decades afterwards the heavily mined barrier went on causing casualties among the local population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> joined the Ottawa Treaty banning landmines, and in 2017 it announced that after decades of work it had met its clearance obligation under the treaty, having cleared its last 93 mined or suspected areas, among them 78 former mine barrages of the Morice Line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morice Line” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of independence from 1954 eventually tied down some 400,000 or more French troops, many of them conscripts, against the FLN’s National Liberation Army, and saw the fortified and electrified <b>Morice Line</b> built along the Tunisian border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
